rewrote experiments/results/tessera_vs_exl3_followups.json (summary only)

##### notes on this file, 2026-09-02
The SECOND summary block above (from --summarize-only) is authoritative and
supersedes the first: it keys the EXL3 ladder by tensor rather than by row
position (identical here -- every arm has all six rows, verified -- but the
positional form was a latent misalignment bug), it adds the two sub-cap
Tessera-4 rungs imported from tessera8_targets.json after proving the shared
witness arm agrees to 0.0 per tensor, and it compares the reproduction arms at
the precision that document publishes (4 decimals) with the drift recorded.
The first block's "Tessera-8 R=4 per-channel FAIL" is that drift: +6.3e-6 on
the mean out leg, at most 5.4e-5 per tensor, 1.3e-7 in weight space -- the
encoders are deterministic within a process (the per-channel arm is asserted
bit-identical to per_channel_tcq at run time) but not across processes, and the
R=4 alternating LS/Viterbi refit lands a few ties the other way.  R=5 and every
other reproduction arm agree to <= 4.3e-6.  The EXL3 K=4 acceptance check was
never loosened and passes at 5 decimals on all three legs.

##### Gridbook +LDLQ(gated): the certificate is an in-window win (2026-09-02)
experiments/cb_ldlq_window_diagnostic.py, L42.gate_proj FP8-CB K32, same fields, same
nvfp4_cb_reconstruct call the gate itself uses:

    weight rel-err     raw 0.09604   ldlq 0.14686      (+53%)
    first 7168 rows    raw 0.06229   ldlq 0.04260      ratio 0.684
    last  1024 rows    raw 0.06689   ldlq 0.08831      ratio 1.320

so the gate is not lying -- its 0.728 holdout ratio is reproduced (0.684 on the
whole fit window) -- but its holdout is a content-keyed random half of the SAME
first-7168 rows (_ldlq_holdout_split, nvfp4_cb_formats.py:2635), which shares
the fit window's column space and cannot see window shift.  The candidate it
ships is fit on 3584 rows of a 4096-column Hessian, i.e. rank-deficient by
>= 512 directions with only LDLQ_DAMPING_FRACTION = 0.01 (nvfp4_cb_formats.py:
1965) holding it together, and the 53% weight-space blowup is the signature.
This is the same regime tessera-ldlq-regulariser already measured: too little
regularisation is harmful.  The Tessera C-arms in this file take H from all
7168 rows at sigma in {1, 3} and DO travel to the last-1024 window.
Production is unaffected: run-pipeline.sh:209 sets PRISMAQUANT_CB_LDLQ:=0,
passed at :1984 as --cb-ldlq 0; with no CB_LDLQ_SCOPE default the scope derives
from that bool (export_nvfp4_cb.py:1378) to "none", and
nvfp4_cb_footprint.py:680 _ldlq_for_format returns False for every rung.
